  "text": "How to Vote Absentee in South Carolina\u2019s November 3 General Election - SC Votes - South Carolina Election Commission Skip to content News About SEC Accountability Reports Public Notices Meeting Minutes State Procurement Card Statements SEC Job Vacancies Procurement and Awards South Carolina Election Commision Menu Voters Register to Vote Check Your Voter Registration Update Your Voter Registration Information Get My Sample Ballot Find My Polling Place Students Early Voting - 2026 Statewide General Election Absentee Voting Check Your Absentee Ballot Check Your Provisional Ballot Military & Overseas Citizens Voters with Disabilities How to Vote Prep for the 2026 Statewide General Election General FAQs Elections & Statistics Election Results Upcoming Elections Rules for Municipal Elections Special Elections General Election Calendars Electoral College Election Audits Voter Registration and Participation Statistics Training Poll Manager Information Apply to be a Poll Manager Online Training Handbook & Resources Candidates Candidate Information Candidate Tracking System Campaign Finance & Ethics Political Parties Resources Precincts and Polling Places Precinct & District Maps Election Law Changes Municipal Election Commission Resources List Sales Help America Vote Act Vulnerability Disclosure Program Voter Education Materials Election Integrity in South Carolina Contact County Voter Registration & Election Offices Report Fraud State Election Commission Submit a Comment or Complaint FOIA Requests About the SEC Accountability Reports Annual Reports Meeting Minutes Procurement and Awards State Procurement Card Statements SEC Job Vacancies Public Notices SEC Website Privacy Statement News & Press Releases Search News How to Vote Absentee in South Carolina\u2019s November 3 General Election Posted on October 7, 2026 COLUMBIA, S.C. (October 7, 2026) \u2013 Interested in voting absentee for the November 3 General Election but not sure where to start? The State Election Commission (SEC) is breaking down the absentee voting process, including eligibility requirements and important deadlines voters need to know. Who Can Vote Absentee? Absentee voting allows qualified voters to cast a ballot by mail prior to Election Day. Only voters who have a qualifying reason can vote absentee . Common qualifying reasons can be: Having a physical disability Being 65 and older People with employment obligations which prevent them from voting during early voting hours for the duration of the early voting period, and during the hours the polls are open on Election Day. For a full list of qualifying reasons, visit our absentee voting page on scVOTES.gov. Request an Absentee Application You can submit your request for an application as early as January 1, of the election year. Call, visit, or send your request by U.S. mail to your county voter registration and elections office. You must provide your name, date of birth, and last four digits of your SSN. You will be mailed an application. Complete, Sign and Return the Application Return the application by U.S. mail or personal delivery to your county voter registration and elections office as soon as possible. The deadline to return your application is 5:00 p.m. on Friday, October 23 . You will receive your absentee ballot in the mail after your application is returned. Vote and Return your Absentee Ballot Carefully mark your ballot. Place the ballot in the \u201cballot here-in\u201d envelope and place the \u201cballot here-in\u201d envelope in the return envelope. Be sure to sign the voter\u2019s oath and have your signature witnessed. Anyone age 18 or older can witness your signature. A notary is not necessary. Return your ballot to your county voter registration and elections office or an early voting center either by mail or personal delivery. You must present your Photo ID when returning the ballot in person. For a list of acceptable Photo IDs visit scVOTES.gov. Absentee ballots must be received by your county voter registration and elections office by 7:00 p.m. on Election Day, Tuesday, November 3 . Ballots returned by mail should be mailed no later than one week prior to Election Day to help ensure timely delivery. Visit scVOTES.gov to find information about how immediate family members and authorized representatives can make an absentee request or return a ballot for another person. All requestors are limited to five requests in addition to their own per election. Track the Status of Your Absentee Request and Ballot Check the status of your absentee request and track your ballot online at scVOTES.gov . You can also contact your county voter registration and elections office for updates regarding your absentee ballot. Military and Overseas Citizens Special provisions are available for military and overseas citizens. Go to scVOTES.gov or FVAP.gov for more information. ________________________________________________________________________________ Early Voting is available for all voters! For more information on early voting, click here. Posted in Press Release Back to All News MyscVOTES Check My Registration Get My Sample Ballot Find My Polling Place View My Voting History Log in to MyscVOTES Find Your County Voter Registration & Elections Office Find Yours 3,448,376 Current number of registered voters in South Carolina South Carolina Election Commision Every Vote Matters. Every Vote Counts. NoExcuseSC.gov All logos and trademarks in this site are property of their respective owner. \u00a9 2026 South Carolina State Election Commission. All Rights Reserved. Resources Register to Vote Be a Poll Manager News Early Voting - 2026 Statewide General Election Upcoming Elections Contact Absentee Voting Election Results MyscVOTES Login South Carolina Notable State Documents Award Badge Clearing House Awards, 2x Winner Instagram Twitter Facebook LinkedIn",
